<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Jaquizaca on Typophile Archive</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/jaquizaca/</link><description>Recent content in Jaquizaca on Typophile Archive</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 27 Sep 2013 13:43:00 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/jaquizaca/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Jaquizaca – a jaunty typeface with a serious attitude</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/106542/</link><pubDate>Fri, 27 Sep 2013 13:43:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/106542/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I created &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150414043637/http:/www.myfonts.com/fonts/lamatas/jaquizaca/"&gt;Jaquizaca&lt;/a&gt; in 2001 to make &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150414043637/http:/vimeo.com/74241282"&gt;inter-titles for a TV programme&lt;/a&gt;. After twelve years it was revised and updated with ligatures, contextual alternates and other OpenType features. It supports all the European languages using Latin alphabet.&lt;br&gt;
In 2004 I draw &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150414043637/http:/origin.myfonts.com/s/aw/original/242/0/124175.pdf"&gt;a snakes&amp;rsquo;n&amp;rsquo;ladders board&lt;/a&gt; to illustrate a magazine article on graphic design process. Now, with a little help of my friends, I translated it into &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150414043637/http:/origin.myfonts.com/s/aw/original/242/0/124178.pdf"&gt;English&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150414043637/http:/origin.myfonts.com/s/aw/original/242/0/124177.pdf"&gt;Spanish&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20150414043637/http:/origin.myfonts.com/s/aw/original/242/0/124176.pdf"&gt;German&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
